ertices are ","type":"text"},{"code":"0..<vertexCount","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and the columns,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the hyperedges, are at positions ","type":"text"},{"code":"0..<hyperedgeCount","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". It conforms to ","type":"text"},{"code":"Hypergraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", so the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"Hypergraphs","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" algorithms run on it unchanged; it is not a ","type":"text"},{"code":"Graph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" even when built from one,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"because a cell edit can give a column any number of bits.","type":"text"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"IncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/IncidenceMatrix","title":"IncidenceMatrix","url":"\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/incidencematrix","role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"IncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/DirectedHypergraph":{"abstract":[{"text":"A directed hypergraph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of hyperedges, each an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ordered pair of vertex sets, its sources (tail) and its targets (head). Gallo, Longo, Pallottino","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and Nguyen, “Directed hypergraphs and applications”, ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Discrete Applied Mathematics"}]},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"42(2–3):177–201, 1993, define a hyperarc as “an ordered pair … of (possibly empty) disjoint","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"subsets”; here either side may be empty and a vertex may be on both, the hypergraph form of a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"te